---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/apps/brevo";
const title = "Brevo for Shopify: separate campaign permission from service delivery";
const desc = "Evaluate Brevo PushOwl imports, marketing and transactional SMS boundaries, queued-message controls and channel costs.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does Brevo offer SMS?", "answer": "Brevo supports marketing and transactional SMS. Verify the product path, destination availability, sender requirements and plan for the messages you intend to send."}, {"question": "Does the Shopify connection preserve every old status?", "answer": "Do not assume so. Brevo warns that Shopify imports can overwrite contact attributes including subscription status. Reconcile existing suppressions in an isolated rehearsal."}, {"question": "Does an order update permit a promotional text?", "answer": "No. Keep the service purpose and marketing permission separate and verify the actual content, recipient eligibility and sending path."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/apps">All app reviews</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <h2>Current product scope</h2>
    <p>Brevo can serve a store that wants coordinated marketing and a separately governed service-message path. The useful decision is which integration owns each job. These are proposed evaluation exercises, not observed merchant tests or revenue results.</p>
    <h2>Shopify integration and account choice</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: the <a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo PushOwl guide</a> identifies PushOwl as the supported Shopify connection, replacing the former app. It supports email and SMS marketing alongside other channels. Its import warning matters: Shopify can overwrite existing Brevo attributes, including subscription status. Review account selection and permission mapping before importing into an established account.</p>
    <h2>Marketing and transactional are separate decisions</h2>
    <p>Brevo documents <a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/209450925-What-type-of-SMS-messages-can-I-send">bulk marketing SMS and individually triggered informational SMS</a>. A service API is not evidence that a Shopify app automatically sends every order notification. Inventory the installed app, any API integration and Shopify notifications separately. Keep marketing offers out of essential service templates; classify the actual content and purpose before choosing a sending path.</p>
    <h2>Proposed import rehearsal</h2>
    <p>Use an isolated sample with an email-only shopper, a separately SMS-permitted shopper, a previously unsubscribed Brevo contact and a Shopify customer whose attributes disagree. Save source permission evidence, timestamps, destination status and the import outcome. Stop if an old suppression becomes eligible without fresh evidence. Do not treat a phone number, an order, or an account merge as marketing permission. Repeat after a later Shopify update to establish ongoing precedence rather than checking only the first import.</p>
    <h2>Proposed event and service rehearsal</h2>
    <p>Create one test order with two shipments. Define order ID, event ID, parcel ID and event time for each service update. Replay an event, deliver an older status after a newer one, then cancel one parcel. Require a documented deduplication rule and stale-event rule before live sending. Decide which system owns the customer-facing status so Shopify and a custom Brevo service integration do not both notify for the same event. These controls are acceptance requirements to verify, not claims of native app behavior.</p>
    <h2>When a promotion waits</h2>
    <p>Queue a cart campaign, then place the order, withdraw SMS permission and open a delivery complaint in separate test runs. Inspect the queued message at dispatch, not just the contact record. Define a support pause for promotions while retaining necessary order help through an appropriate service route. Verify the actual sender and destination opt-out behavior; record unknown timezone and failed-delivery handling. A marketing withdrawal must not silently become permission for a promotional service message.</p>
    <h2>Back-in-stock scope</h2>
    <p>The integration guide describes an email product-alert widget. Use it to evaluate variant-specific demand without assuming the same widget grants SMS permission or that all channels share that trigger. Subscribe to two variants, restock only one, purchase before dispatch and restock again. Record variant identity, eligibility and duplicate-alert behavior. Add SMS only after verifying the trigger, channel permission and purchased-variant exit in the connected setup.</p>
    <h2>Economic worksheet</h2>
    <p>Separate the PushOwl channel plan, any Brevo messaging/API usage and connector or engineering work. Obtain current destination and segment charges for the selected sending path; do not infer that one subscription covers every product. Model a normal campaign month and a disruption month with many service updates. Include replay prevention and incident support time: cheap sends are not useful if duplicate events multiply them.</p>
    <h2>Measure the campaign without withholding service</h2>
    <p>Randomize eligible marketing contacts into campaign and holdout groups before sending; preserve essential service for both groups. Compare net sales per assigned contact after the same return window, then subtract cost of goods, fulfillment, messaging and incremental support costs. Discounts and refunds already reflected in net sales are not deducted again. Report delivery failures and permission exclusions separately. Allocate fixed fees separately from incremental campaign costs; the worksheet does not establish a price winner.</p>
    <h2>Choose the next step</h2>
    <p>Proceed when import precedence preserves permissions, each notification has one owner and the selected plan supports the required channels. Keep an existing service sender if it already passes the event exercises; consolidation is optional. If import reconciliation fails, resolve the source-of-truth mapping before migrating the full audience. Review <a href="/guides/consent">permission capture</a>, <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> and <a href="/guides/pricing">cost planning</a> with your actual implementation.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
